+
+# WelcomePi updates
+
+## Choose the HDMI socket on a Pi 4B or Pi 5
+
+Run `sudoedit /etc/welcomepi.env` on the kiosk. Set `WELCOMEPI_HDMI_PORT=0` for the socket marked **HDMI0** or `WELCOMEPI_HDMI_PORT=1` for **HDMI1**. Save the file and run `sudo systemctl restart welcomepi.service`. The default, `auto`, uses the first connected HDMI display. [Pi 4 and Pi 5 installation](https://github.com/ManchesterMakerspace/makerspace-rails-2026/blob/master/tools/WelcomePi/README.md#install-on-a-pi-4-or-pi-5)
+
+If the board stays blank, check its input and cable. Follow `sudo journalctl -b -u welcomepi.service -f` on the Pi for a warning naming the disconnected socket and a recovery message when its connection returns. These messages also reach the configured syslog destination. From `~/WelcomePi`, run `sudo bash setup.sh check` for the current HDMI status.
+
+## Choose which doors open a welcome
+
+On the kiosk, edit `/etc/welcomepi.env` and restart `welcomepi.service` after saving. Set `WELCOMEPI_LOCATION_PATTERNS` to a JSON list of case-insensitive regexes, for example `WELCOMEPI_LOCATION_PATTERNS='["^Main Entrance$","^Lobby Reader-"]'`. A fresh name-only check-in from a matching location shows the large first name immediately. When the check-in or a fuller record of that tap supplies the card UID, the kiosk looks up the card in its directory or queries MongoDB in the background. It uses the card’s assigned member ID to open the eligible member’s agenda automatically. If the greeting stays on screen, the member can tap their own active fob locally. Private details require that local tap. The final segment of an activity topic identifies the sending reader. The number after the payload's operation is its publication timestamp. An open private details page keeps priority over a name-only greeting. For the location match the kiosk reads the document's `where` field, then `reader`, then `blank:` if both are missing, even when the sending reader is known. Leave the setting unset to allow every door, or set it to `[]` to keep all MQTT arrivals history-only.
+
+Every location contributes to the rolling 16-hour history. A filtered door is logged and can update the member’s time on **Recent check-ins**. Local fob taps still open eligible welcomes. Give the kiosk broker account read access to `activity/checkins/#` and `checkins/insert` before rollout. Run `sudo bash setup.sh watch-door` from `~/WelcomePi` and check in at a matching door to confirm the installation.
+
+## Adjust screen layout and dimming
+
+The public board changes its colors and layout when it opens and every seven minutes while it stays visible. It alternates light and dark and moves the portal QR through all four corners. Pages with room show a blank row between recent check-ins and volunteer lists. The QR can double in width and height, extending beyond the colored title bar wherever the corner is clear on every page. It stays in place throughout the eight-second page cycle. Greetings, agendas and details change layout on the next visit and stay at normal brightness. Set `WELCOMEPI_LAYOUT_INTERVAL_MINUTES` in `/etc/welcomepi.env` to change the board interval. Set `WELCOMEPI_DIM_AFTER_MINUTES` to change how long the board waits after the last fresh check-in or local fob tap before dimming. The default is 15 minutes. Use positive whole minutes for both settings and run `sudo systemctl restart welcomepi.service` after editing.
+
+Set `WELCOMEPI_OPPORTUNITY_MIN_POINTS` in `/etc/welcomepi.env` to choose the minimum points per opportunity on member agendas. The default is 1.0 and accepts fractional overrides. If text is cut off, set `WELCOMEPI_INSET` in pixels for all edges, or adjust just the clipped edges with `WELCOMEPI_INSET_TOP`, `WELCOMEPI_INSET_RIGHT`, `WELCOMEPI_INSET_BOTTOM` and `WELCOMEPI_INSET_LEFT`. These settings follow the upright picture. For heavier text, start with `WELCOMEPI_FONT_STROKE=1`. Set these in `/etc/welcomepi.env` and restart `welcomepi.service`.
+
+A fresh check-in at any door or a distinct tap on the local reader restores normal brightness while HDMI is awake. This includes history-only doors and unrecognized local fobs. Normal welcome and membership rules still determine which page opens. Two minutes without qualifying activity clear personal details. After four hours without qualifying activity HDMI enters standby until a qualifying scan.
+
+## Enable automatic updates
+
+Kiosk operators can enroll a working entrance display in automatic updates. Complete a local fob tap and a door check-in first. On the Pi, run `sudo bash setup.sh enable-updates` from `~/WelcomePi`. The Pi checks for a tested release about every five minutes. [Update enrollment and recovery guide](https://github.com/ManchesterMakerspace/makerspace-rails-2026/blob/master/tools/WelcomePi/setup-pi0.MD#8-enable-automatic-updates-from-master)
+
+Run `systemctl list-timers welcomepi-update.timer` to see the next check. To check immediately, run `sudo systemctl start welcomepi-update.service`. A new app release briefly restarts the display and returns it to the generic board. The kiosk keeps running through a failed download and restores its previous version after a failed startup check.
+
+For an update problem, inspect `sudo journalctl -b -u welcomepi-update.service`. The setup guide covers held releases and pausing updates for host maintenance. After a successful update, confirm one welcome at the kiosk reader and another from a real door check-in.
+
+## Reader and scan logs
+
+After a restart, look for the PN532 provisioning entry in `sudo journalctl -b -t welcomepi` or on the kiosk's configured remote syslog collector. The kiosk tries I²C, then UART. If neither responds, door check-ins still operate the display. Each scan entry identifies the reader and, when verified, the member. For MQTT arrivals, DEBUG confirms receipt, INFO records a welcome or accepted scan, WARNING explains a skipped welcome, and ERROR identifies a member lookup failure or missing card UID. MQTT and MongoDB errors include the driver reason. Use these records to investigate a reported scan at the time and door the member gives you.
